<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Barby</h1><p class="st-meta">1497 CE – 1659 CE · capital Barby</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small county on the Elbe near Magdeburg, ruled by the counts of Barby until their line died out in 1659.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The lords of Barby held lands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-anhalt">Anhalt</a>, from the 12th century. In 1497 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-i-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Maximilian I</a> raised them to imperial counts, though much of their territory was held in fief from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/archbishopric-of-magdeburg">Archbishopric of Magdeburg</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts ruled a few small towns and villages, including Barby, Rosenburg and Mühlingen, from their castle at Barby. They introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Lutheran Reformation</a> in the 1540s. The territory lived from farming in the fertile Elbe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a> plain and from river trade.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When the last count, August Ludwig, died in 1659, the county was divided among his overlords: most of it went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> dukes of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-weissenfels">Duchy of Saxe-Weissenfels</a>, and some lands to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-anhalt-zerbst">Anhalt-Zerbst</a>. Barby became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1815.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-barby.jpg" alt="County of Barby" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Mazbln (talk · contribs) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barby_Rathaus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1497 CE – 1659 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Barby</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>August Ludwig of Barby</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Barby</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1578&amp;focus=county-of-barby" aria-label="County of Barby on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4948 382 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Barby lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5268 638L5229 694M5268 638L5312 664"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5312 664h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5268 638h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5268 638h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1578&amp;focus=county-of-barby">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
